 ## Background
-The Digital Differential Analyzer (DDA) for grid traversal is a classic algorithm in computer graphics, originally described by Amanatides and Woo in 1987 [S1, S2, unofficial]. It is widely used in ray tracing and volume rendering for efficient traversal of uniform grids. The method is implemented in various open-source projects, including Taylor-NeRF and CNC [S1, S2, unofficial].
+The Digital Differential Analyzer (DDA) for grid traversal is a classic algorithm in computer graphics that leverages the orthogonality of the grid to determine the next neighboring voxel the ray intersects [S1, unofficial]. It initializes parameters for a 3D DDA to traverse a voxel grid along a ray, computing step directions, initial indices, and distances to voxel boundaries [S3, unofficial].
 
